To start your private chain:
1. Please double click the "Private Chain Kickstart" icon
2. When the chain boots up, please enter the following command to enable smart mining.
This small program allows the client to mine only if there's any transaction left.
loadScript("/home/devlechain/Tools/smartMine.js")
3. If you want to stop the private chain, please type exit to terminate the program.

To directly test the message sending under Geth
1. Ensure your private chain is booted.
2. When the chain boots up, please enter the following command to load the guiding program
loadScript("/home/devlechain/Tools/loadMessage/Message.js")
3. Follow the on-screen guide to perform get() and set() function.

To start the message sending over blockchain program
1. Ensure your private chain is booted.
2. Double click the ""Eclipse" icon.
3. When the window "Select a directory as workspace" appears, click Launch.
4. Click "messageApp" on the project explorer.
5. Navigate src -> messageApp -> Messenger.java and double click.
6. Click Run->Run on the top menu, or press Ctrl + F11 to evaluate.
7. Interact with the program on the console area.

To start the Bank smart contract application
1. Ensure your private chain is booted.
2. Double click the ""Eclipse" icon.
3. When the window "Select a directory as workspace" appears, click Launch.
4. Click "bankApp" on the project explorer.
5. Navigate src -> bankApp -> BankMain.java and double click.
6. Click Run->Run on the top menu, or press Ctrl + F11 to evaluate.
7. Interact with the program on the console area.

To start the PoW experiment application
1. Double click the ""Eclipse" icon.
2. When the window "Select a directory as workspace" appears, click Launch.
3. Click "simplePoW" on the project explorer.
4. Navigate src -> simplePoW -> SimplePoW.java and double click.
5. Click Run->Run on the top menu, or press Ctrl + F11 to evaluate.
6. Interact with the program on the console area.

To debug a contract
1. Double click the "Remix IDE" icon.
2. On the left side, find "contract" folder, and list the folder
3. Click on the contract you would like to debug.
4. After fixing your code, click "Solidity Compiler" on the left side panel.
5. Compile your contract.
6. If the compiler finishes your code without any issue, click "Deploy and Run Transaction"
7. You can test your contract by clicking the function you have proposed in your smart contract.

To update your Ethereum and its toolset to the latest version
1. Ensure that the virtual environment is connected to the Internet
2. Run /home/devlechain/Applications/Ethereum/ethUpdater -u

To update your Eclipse IDE and its toolset to the latest version:
1. Ensure that the virtual environment is connected to the Internet
2. Run /home/devlechain/Applications/Eclipse/ecUpdater -u

If you like to create an manage your own Chain:
1. Open a termianl, switch to /home/devlechain/ChainData directory:
2. Run the following command:
   createChain
   initChain
   bootChain
   removeChain
   All of these command support arguments and interactive interface, 
   When no arguments are given, the program will print out help information.
   
Important note on using bootChain:
Some functions of bootChain is still under heavy development, in which that after a chain is inited, you have to explicitly tell bootChain to make the "inited Chain" bootable:

Supposed that you have just inited a chain with ID 30000, to make the chain bootable with network ID 30000:

	./bootChain -b 30000 -w I -s "network_id=30000"

In this case, the special network ID "I" is a invalid network ID,
However, it is the flag to tell the program that such chain is just inited and have never being booted.

You only have to do this step once for each newly created chain. 
After that, you don't have to use -w I again.

Have fun.

